## 1. Introduction
To realize sustainable technological development, it is essential to design renewable power sources. LIBs have attracted considerable attention as a stable energy storage system because of their high energy density and conversion efficiency, which makes them ideal candidates [1–5]. The theoretical capacity of graphite electrode materials for use in commercial LIBs is relatively low (372 $\mathrm{mAh}/\mathrm{g}$ , maximal Li uptake $\mathrm{LiC}_{6}^{\cdot}$ ). Therefore, it is imperative and important to explore and develop new anode materials with higher theoretical capacity. At present, several types of anode materials are available for LIBs, including alloys [6,7], carbon-based materials [8,9], metal oxides [10,11], metal sulfides [12,13], and metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) [14,15]. Metal oxides have a high theoretical capacity, which is a beneficial characteristic for their application in next-generation LIBs. However, the practical application of metal oxides is still limited because of their fast capacity fading, low rate performance, low intrinsic electric conductivity, and short life cycle caused by large volume changes during the lithiation and delithiation processes. To solve these issues, hybrid materials composed of transition metal oxides and various other components are used [16–18]. Among these, composites fabricated through the integration of two or more transition metal oxides are the most effective materials for LIB application. The purpose of hybridization is to combine the advantages of each component and to enhance the physicochemical properties of metal oxides, such as electrochemical reactivity and mechanical stability [19,20].
MOFs are typically porous materials composed of metal nodes and organic linkers [21–23]. Transition metal oxides based on MOF precursors with controlled architectures and chemical compositions, such as $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ [24], CuO [25], $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ [26], NiO [27], $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}.$ $\mathrm{O_{4}/N i O}$ [28], $\mathsf{N i F e}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ , $Z\mathrm{nFe}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}$ , and ${\mathsf{C o F e}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}$ [29], have attracted considerable research interest. Among these materials, nanostructured $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ demonstrated excellent electrochemical properties because of its high theoretical capacity $(890\mathrm{\mAh/g)}$ and low lithium intercalation potential [30]. It has been proven that nanocrystallization can shorten the Li-ion migration path and accommodate the volume change during the cycling process, thus improving the storage performance. Ding et al. reported amorphous $\mathrm{Co}_{3}0_{4}@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ hollow composites prepared via thermal decomposition of zeolitic imidazolate framework-67 (ZIF-67) and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ coating; these composites exhibited a significantly high cycling performance $(1057\ \mathrm{mAh/g}$ at $100~\mathrm{{mA/g}}$ after 100 cycles) as anodes for LIBs [31]. Zhong and co-workers synthesized nanostructured $\mathrm{Co}_{3}{\sf O}_{4}–\mathrm{CoFe}_{2}{\sf O}_{4}$ composites through one-step pyrolysis of bimetallic MOFs [32]. When these composites were used as anodes for LIBs, their excellent electrochemical performance was mainly attributed to the well distribution and small size of the particles. In short, the cycling stability and electrochemical performance of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ can be improved through reasonable hybridization with other metal oxides. ${\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ is a well-known catalyst in the field of photocatalysis and its degradation properties are significantly enhanced when combined with $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ because of the formation of p-n heterogeneous junctions in $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C e O_{2}}$ [33,34]. Therefore, ${\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ can be used to buffer the volume change of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ , and the $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C e O_{2}}$ heterostructure can be used as an anode material for LIBs. However, the electrochemical performance of the ${C o_{3}}{0_{4}}/$ ${\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ composite has not been widely investigated.
Herein, a $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}}/\mathrm{CeO_{2}}$ heterostructure was fabricated from a bimetallic MOF precursor and applied as an anode material for LIBs. In the $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C e O_{2}}$ heterostructure, ${\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ can limit the volume expansion of $C0_{3}0_{4},$ preventing the electrode from cracking. Benefiting from the unique structure and synergistic effect of the bimetallic composition, $5{\mathsf{C o}}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}/{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ displayed excellent energy storage performance, especially with respect to the rate capacity, and, thus, was shown to be applicable as an anode material for LIBs.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Materials
Polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP, K30), cobalt nitrate hexahydrate (Co $(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathsf{H}_{2}0)$ , and cerium nitrate hexahydrate $(\mathsf{C e}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{3}.6\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})$ with a purity of $99.99\%$ were purchased from Sinopharm. Pyrazole-3,5-dicarboxylic acid hydrate $\mathrm{\cdot}\mathrm{\mathrm{H}}_{3}\mathrm{pdc}$ , $\mathrm{C}_{5}\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{4}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $98\%$ and N, N-dimethylformamide (DMF, $99.5\%$ were supplied by Alfa Aesar. All above-mentioned chemicals were used directly without further purification.
## 2.2. Preparation of precursors
$\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\ \mathrm{H}_{2}0$ (1 mmol) and $\mathsf{C e}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}6\ \mathsf{H}_{2}0$ (1 mmol) were dissolved under stirring in $15~\mathrm{mL}$ DMF to attain a purple solution (solution A). Pyrazole-3,5-dicarboxylic acid hydrate $(0.348\ \mathrm{g})$ and PVP $(0.300\:\mathrm{g})$ were stirred in $15~\mathrm{mL}$ DMF until a clear solution (solution B) was obtained. Solution A was slowly poured into solution B, and then agitated for $0.5\mathrm{~h~}$ until uniformly mixed. Next, the mixed solution was loaded into a microwave digester and reacted at $100^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^{1\mathrm{~h~}}$ . After cooling to room temperature, the sample was collected by centrifugation. Then, the sample was dried at $80~^{\circ}C$ overnight to obtain the Co-Ce metal-organic framework (Co-Ce-MOF) precursor. By adjusting the ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}/{\mathsf{C e}}^{3+}$ molar ratio, 3Co-Ce-MOFs, 5Co-Ce-MOFs, 7Co-Ce-MOFs, and Co-MOFs with molar ratios of 3:1, 5:1, 7:1, and 1:0 (Co:Ce), respectively, were prepared following a similar method as described above.
## 2.3. Synthesis of $C o_{3}O_{4}/C e O_{2}$
The $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}}/\mathrm{CeO_{2}}$ composites were generated by calcining Co-CeMOFs. In brief, the Co-Ce-MOF precursor was dispersed in a crucible and then calcined in air at $500^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ for $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ to generate a black powder. Similarly, Co-Ce-MOF with the above-mentioned molar ratios were subjected to the same calcination conditions. Finally, nanostructured $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ , $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C e O_{2}}$ , $3{\mathsf{C o}}_{3}0_{4}/{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ , $5{\mathsf{C o}}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}/{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ , and $7\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}}/\mathrm{CeO_{2}}$ were obtained.
## 2.4. Material characterization
The morphology and microstructure of these samples were determined with field emission scanning electron microscopy (FE-SEM, Hitachi SU8010) and transmission electron microscopy (TEM, JEM-2010F). Powder X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns of the samples were collected by a MiniFlex600 diffractometer. The structures of the as-prepared MOFs were analyzed by infrared spectroscopy (Prestige-21 Jindao). Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) was performed on a thermal analyzer (NETZSCH STA 449F3) in air at a heating rate of $10~\mathrm{^{\circ}C/m i n}$ . X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was conducted to determine the chemical valence states of samples using a scanning X-ray microprobe (Thermo SCIENTIFIC ESCALAB 250Xi). Nitrogen adsorptiondesorption isotherms were recorded at $77~\mathrm{K}$ using an automated volumetric adsorption analyzer (Beijing JWGB Tech., JWBK122W). The specific surface area (SSA) was calculated by the Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) method, based on the adsorption data over the relative pressure $\left({p/p_{0}}\right)$ range of $0.01\substack{-0.10}$ .
## 2.5. Electrochemical tests
The electrochemical performance of all samples was evaluated using CR2032-type half cells. The working electrode was composed of $70\%$ active material, $20\%$ acetylene black, and $10\%$ polyvinylidene fluoride (PVDF). The mixed slurry was evenly coated on a copper foil and then dried at $60^{\circ}\mathsf C$ under vacuum for $15\mathrm{h}$ . The weight of the electrode material on the copper foil was $1.2\pm0.2~\mathrm{mg/cm}^{2}$ . Lithium metal and a Celgard 2400 film were used as the counter electrode and separator, respectively. $\mathrm{LiPF}_{6}$ (1 M) dissolved in ethylene carbonate (EC)/dimethyl carbonate (DMC)/diethyl carbonate (DEC) (EC/DMC/DEC 1:1:1 $\mathbf{v}/\mathbf{v})$ was used as the electrolyte. A LAND CT2001 battery testing system was applied to examine the galvanostatic charge and discharge (GCD) and the rate performance of electrode materials within the voltage range of $0.01{-}3.0\mathrm{~V~}$ . An electrochemical workstation (CHI660E, Shanghai Chenhua) was utilized to acquire cyclic voltammetry (CV) profile from 0.01 to $3.00\mathrm{~V~}$ at a scan rate of $0.1~\mathrm{mV}/s$ and electrochemical impedance spectroscopy in the frequency range from $0.01~\mathrm{Hz}$ to $100~\mathrm{KHz}$ .
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Material characterization
Compared with the common solvothermal method, the microwave method has attracted considerable attention because of its advantages such as fast heating and high thermal energy efficiency. Scheme 1 illustrates the preparation $\mathrm{Co}_{3}0_{4}/\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ . The Co-Ce-MOFs materials were prepared following a one-step microwave strategy using H3pdc as the ligand, followed by calcination under air for $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ to obtain the mesoporous $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}}/\mathrm{CeO_{2}}$ heterostructure. XRD analysis was performed to identify the structural characteristics of the samples. Fig. 1a reveals the existence of two phases of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and ${\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ . The intensity of the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ peaks increased with increasing molar ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}/{\mathsf{C e}}^{3+}$ ratio, while that of the ${\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ peak decreased. The XRD peaks at $19.0^{\circ}$ , $31.3^{\circ}$ , $36.8^{\circ}$ , $38.5^{\circ}$ , $44.8^{\circ}$ , $59.4^{\circ}$ , and $65.2^{\circ}$ were assigned to the (111), (220), (311), (222), (400), (511), and (440) planes of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ (JCPDS: 43-1003), respectively. Additionally, other peaks at $28.6^{\circ}$ , $47.5^{\circ}$ , $56.3^{\circ}$ , and $76.7^{\circ}$ corresponded to the (111), (220), (311), and (331) planes of ${\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ (JCPDS:34-0394), respectively. Infrared spectroscopy (IR) was used to characterize the structure and functional groups of the ligands. As shown in Fig. S1, the absorption peaks at $1350{-}1662\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were attributed to asymmetric and symmetric stretching vibrations of the coordinated carboxylate groups [35]. The intense bands at 1017– $1110~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were assigned to the stretching vibration of the $C{\mathrm{-}}C$ groups, and the absorption peaks appearing at $788-840c m^{-1}$ were assigned to the $C{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{H}$ external bending vibration. The thermal behavior of Co-MOFs and 5Co-Ce-MOFs was analyzed under air by using TG curves (Fig. S2 and Fig. 1b). As shown in Fig. 1b, the initial mass loss between 62 and $220^{\circ}\mathsf C$ is attributed to the evaporation of solvent molecules (DMF) trapped in the pores of 5Co-CeMOFs [36,37]. The weight loss between 220 and $360^{\circ}\mathsf C$ was potentially caused by the decomposition of the organic ligand. Notably, no weight loss was observed at above $360~^{\circ}\mathsf C$ , and $5{\mathsf{C o}}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}/{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ residues accounted for $37.21\%$ , indicating the complete oxidation of 5Co-Ce-MOFs. $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption-desorption curves were used to analyze the pore distribution and SSA of the prepared samples (Fig. 1c-e). The results show that the increase in the molar ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}/$ $\mathsf{C e}^{3+}$ ratio is attributable for the increase in SSA. As shown in Fig. 1c, the SSA of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ was only $4.542~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ . The molar ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}/$ ${\mathsf{C e}}^{3+}$ ratio of 7:1 provided the maximum SSA of $27.01\ \mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ . The SSAs of $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C e O_{2}}$ , $3{\mathsf{C o}}_{3}0_{4}/{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ , and $5{\mathsf{C o}}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}/{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ were 4.858, 16.706, and $19.265~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}.$ , respectively. All isotherms exhibited an approximate type IV isotherm with a hysteresis loop in the $p/p_{0}$ range of $_{0.6-1.0}$ (Fig. 1d and e), which was related to the adsorption and desorption in the mesoporous structure by capillary condensation [25]. The pore size distribution in the range from 2 to $50\ \mathrm{nm}$ was representative of mesopores (insert of Fig. 1d and e). This porosity facilitates the diffusion of electrolytes and shortens the ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ transport path. In addition to conversion reaction, the SSA can provide the adsorption capacity. An interface storage mechanism has been previously proposed to improve $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ storage capacity [38,39].
Furthermore, the sample morphologies were observed through FE-SEM. As depicted in Fig. 2, all samples show a homogeneous morphology composed of irregular small blocks. The $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ material had a diameter of $1{-}2\ \upmu\mathrm{m}$ and a rough surface (Fig. 2f). $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C e O_{2}}$ , $3{\mathsf{C o}}_{3}0_{4}/{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ , and $5{\mathsf{C o}}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}/{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ displayed diameters of $200{-}300~\mathrm{{nm}}$ and rough surfaces (Fig. 2g-i). The $7\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}}/\mathrm{CeO_{2}}$ sample had a small particle size of only $100\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 2j). These results indicate that $5{\mathsf{C o}}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}/{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ has a more uniform morphology. The microscopic morphologies of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and $5{\mathsf{C o}}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}/{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ were investigated through TEM, as shown in Fig. 3a and d. Compared with $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ , $5{\mathsf{C o}}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}/{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ exhibited a smaller particle size and a uniform morphology. High-resolution electron microscopy (HRTEM) was used to characterize the composition and structure of different samples. Fig. 3b shows a regular and clear lattice fringe pattern, indicating that the synthesized $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ consisted of a single phase. The characteristic spacings of 0.24, 0.28, and $0.46~\mathrm{{nm}}$ corresponded to the (311), (220), and (111) lattice planes of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4},$ respectively. The regular diffraction spots in Fig. 3c confirmed the single crystal structure of pristine $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ . As seen in the HRTEM image of $5{\mathsf{C o}}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}/{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ (Fig. 3e), the interlaced fringes revealed a combination of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ . The selected area electron diffraction (SAED) patterns displayed spacings of $0.19\ \mathrm{nm}$ corresponding to (2 2 0) lattice planes of ${\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ (Fig. 3f). Moreover, the lattice distances of 0.14 and $0.24\ \mathrm{nm}$ were assigned to the (440) and (311) planes of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ , respectively. The circular diffraction pattern indicated that $5{\mathsf{C o}}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}/{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ had a polycrystalline structure. The elemental mapping (Fig. 3g-i) images clearly demonstrate the uniform elemental distribution of Co and Ce in $5{\mathsf{C o}}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}/{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ .
The chemical valence states of the surface elements in $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and $5{\mathsf{C o}}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}/{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ were further analyzed by XPS. The survey spectrum of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ shows the characteristic peaks of Co, C, and O, while the spectrum of $5{\mathsf{C o}}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}/{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ displays Ce, Co, C, and O (Fig. 4a). Fig. 4b exhibits the Ce 3d spectrum, which is deconvoluted to eight peaks marked as $\alpha_{1}$ , $\alpha_{2}$ , $\alpha_{3}$ , $\alpha_{4}$ $\beta_{1}$ , b2, $\beta_{3}$ , and $\beta_{4},$ respectively. The peaks of $\alpha_{2}$ and $\beta_{2}$ represented the $3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ of the ${\mathsf{C e}}^{3+}$ states, whereas the other six peaks were attributed to the $3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ feature of the ${\mathsf{C e}}^{4+}$ fractions [40,41]. These results indicated that Ce exists in a mixed valence state in $5{\mathsf{C o}}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}/{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ . As shown in Fig. 4c and d, the peak positions of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and $5{\mathsf{C o}}_{3}0_{4}/{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ were almost unchanged, indicating that the Co valence state was consistent. The spectrum of Co $\mathsf{2p}$ exhibits two distinct peaks located at 794.7 and 779.6 eV, corresponding to the energy separation between Co $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and Co $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ , which was $\mathord{\sim}15\ \mathrm{eV}$ (Fig. 4d). Data analysis confirmed the coexistence of $C0^{2+}$ and ${\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}$ and the successful synthesis of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ [20,42,43]. The C 1s spectrum of $5{\mathsf{C o}}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}/{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ is shown in Fig. S3. The characteristic peaks of CAC, CAO, and $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ bonds appeared at approximately 284.8, 285.9, and 288.9 eV, respectively.
## 3.2. Electrochemical properties
The CV and GCD experiments were conducted to explore the electrochemical properties of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and $5{\mathsf{C o}}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}/{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ . Fig. 5a and c display the CV profiles of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and $5{\mathsf{C o}}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}/{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ at $0.1~\mathrm{mV}/s$ . As shown in Fig. 5a, the two peaks at 0.63 and $0.90{\mathrm{V}}$ were respectively associated with the formation of the solid electrolyte interface (SEI) film and the reduction of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ to Co metal nanoparticles in the initial discharging process [44]. During the following cycles, the areas of the redox peaks significantly decreased and became asymmetric, indicating that a significant irreversible reaction occurred, which reduced the activity. These results indicate that the large volume change of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ during the chargingdischarging processes caused the structural change. As presented in Fig. 5c, the peaks at 0.86 and $0.68\mathrm{V}$ in the initial cathodic sweep were assigned to the reduction of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ to metallic Co as well as the generation of the SEI film. The anodic peak at $2.06~\mathrm{V}$ corresponded to the reversible oxidization of Co to CoO [45,46]. During the following cycles, the cathodic peak at $0.86\mathrm{~V~}$ significantly decreased and shifted because of the reduction of CoO to metallic Co [47]. The CV curves nearly overlapped during the subsequent cycles (2nd and 3rd cycles), which proved the reversible reaction of $5{\mathsf{C o}}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}/{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ . In a previous report [48], the cathodic/anodic peaks of $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ appeared at $0.6/2.5\mathrm{~V~}$ , from which it was extrapolated that $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ does not participate in the reaction of the modified system. Therefore, $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ improved the structural stability of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4},$ and the CV profiles were consistent with the results reported in previous studies [32,43].
Fig. 5b and d show the GCD curves of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and $5{\mathsf{C o}}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}/{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ for 1st, 20th, 50th, and 100th cycles at $100\mathrm{\mA/g}$ . In the first discharging process, discharge potential plateaus at around $1.0\mathrm{V}$ versus $\mathrm{Li/Li^{+}}$ were attributed to the reduction of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ to metallic Co and the formation of the SEI film. Moreover, in the first charging process, the low potential plateaus at ${\sim}2.0\mathrm{V}$ (vs. $\mathrm{Li/Li^{+}})$ were attributed to the slow oxidation of Co. The GCD result was concordant with the CV curves. The initial discharge-charge capacity of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ was $1160.5/873.6\ \mathrm{mAh/g}$ , corresponding to $75.3\%$ of the initial coulombic efficiency (ICE) (Fig. 5b). However, the $5{\mathsf{C o}}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}/{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ sample achieved a high discharge-charge capacity of 1091.0/846.7 $\mathrm{{\mAh/g}}$ , with an ICE of $77.6\%$ (Fig. 5d). In the following cycles of $5{\mathsf{C o}}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}/{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ , the coulombic efficiency increased to ${\sim}98\%$ , displaying improved reversibility and excellent cycling stability. In the second cycle, there was a considerable decrease in discharge capacity possibly because of the generation of the SEI film during the first cycle. In addition, the discharge capacity increased after 50 and 100 cycles, which was mainly attributed to the diffusion of the electrolyte into the active materials and the exposure of more active sites during the cycling process [49].
Fig. 6a shows the cycling performance of all five samples between 0.01 and $3.0\mathrm{V}$ at the current density of $100\mathrm{mA/g}$ . The discharge capacities of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ , $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}/C e O_{2}}$ , $3{\mathsf{C o}}_{3}0_{4}/{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ , $5C_{0_{3}}0_{4}/$ ${\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ , and $7\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}}/\mathrm{CeO_{2}}$ were 538.6, 209.6, 645.4, 1131.2, and $125.5\mathrm{mAh/g}$ after 100 cycles, respectively. Among the composites, $5{\mathsf{C o}}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}/{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ exhibited the highest electrochemical performance, which decreased (to $859.6\ \mathrm{mAh/g)}$ in the second cycle because the electrolyte was oxidized and the SEI film was formed. Subsequently, the capacity of $5{\mathsf{C o}}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}/{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ increased along with the cycles. Capacity increases are common in metal oxide composite anode materials for the following reasons: (1) an activation process is first needed for the diffusion of the electrolyte into active materials and the exposure of more active sites during the charging–discharging process; (2) the oxygen vacancies enhance the intrinsic electronic conductivity of the electrode material, which facilitates the charge transfer for ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ storage [50–52]; (3) the presence of a polymeric electrolyte interphase caused by electrolyte degradation at low voltage provides additional pseudocapacitive lithium storage capacity [38,39,53,54]. However, excessive ${\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ reduces the conductivity of electrode materials, and a larger SSA leads to more side reactions, resulting in a lower capacity. Table 1 summarizes the electrochemical properties of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ -based composites as the anode material that were reported in recent years. Furthermore, Fig. 6b displays the different rate capacities of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and $5C_{0_{3}}0_{4}/$ $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ from 100 to $2000~\mathrm{\mA/g}$ . The $5{\mathsf{C o}}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}/{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ anode material exhibited a capacity of $1388\mathrm{\mAh/g}$ with a current density of $50\ \mathrm{mA/g}$ after five cycles. Increasing the current density to 100, 200, 500, 800, 1000, and $2000\mathrm{\mA/g}$ , resulted in the corresponding discharge capacities of 1371, 1385.9, 1280.0, 1156.8, 1052.5, and $835.3~\mathrm{\mAh/g}$ , respectively. However, when the current rate returned to $50\ \mathrm{mA/g}$ , the reversible capacities were higher than those in the initial five cycles. By contrast, the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ anode material displayed much lower capacities of 770.5, 704.2, 581.6, 405.5, 282.4, and $219.1\ \mathrm{mAh/g}$ under different current rates. Moreover, the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ anode material regained a value of $687.4~\mathrm{mAh/g},$ much lower than that of the $5{\mathsf{C o}}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}/{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ anode material under the same test conditions. Therefore, the above-mentioned phenomenon further confirms the beneficial effect of $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ for improving the stability of the $5{\mathsf{C o}}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}/{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ anode material.
To gain further understanding of the $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ effect on enhanced lithium storage performance, EIS tests were conducted for $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and $5{\mathsf{C o}}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}/{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ . Fig. S4 displays Nyquist plots of the asobtained samples as fresh cells. $R_{\mathrm{ct}}$ represents the charge transfer resistance related to the semicircle of the middle frequency region. The slope of the line in the low frequency region is related to Li-ion diffusion [58]. This result indicates that the presence of ${\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ increases $R_{\mathrm{{ct}}}.$ Among the samples containing ${\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ , $5{\mathsf{C o}}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}/{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ exhibits the steepest slope in the low frequency range, which indicates the fastest Li-ion diffusion. To understand the reasons for the high electrochemical performance of $5{\mathsf{C o}}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}/{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ (Fig. 6a), the Nyquist plots of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and $5{\mathsf{C o}}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}/{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ at $100\ \mathrm{mA/g}$ after 100 cycles are displayed in Fig. 6c. The EIS curves consist of two semicircles and a diagonal line. The equivalent circuit diagram (Fig. 6c) was used to calculate the EIS results, and the fitting parameters are provided in Table 2. The $R_{\mathrm{SEI}}$ of $5{\mathsf{C o}}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}/{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ was $173.4\Omega$ . It was lower than that of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ $(207.5\ \Omega)$ , which explains the easier lithium migration on the interface of SEI. Notably, the $R_{\mathrm{{ct}}}$ of $5{\mathsf{C o}}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}/{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ was much lower $\mathbf{\xi}_{\mathbf{619.1}\ \Omega}$ , Table 2) than that of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ ( $1336\Omega$ , Table 2), which indicated the higher electronic conductivity. The $5{\mathsf{C o}}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}/{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ anode material displayed a steeper slope, which indicates a smaller Li-ion diffusion barrier compared to that formed by the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ anode. These results demonstrate that $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ addition effectively alleviates the volume change of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ during the cycling process, facilitating high electrochemical performance. In addition, Nyquist plots of the other samples are shown in Fig. S5. The long-term cycling performance of $5{\mathsf{C o}}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}/{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ at a high current density of $500~\mathrm{{mA/g}}$ was further tested. As depicted in Fig. S6, $5{\mathsf{C o}}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}/{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ exhibits a discharge capacity of 901.4 $\mathrm{mAh}/\mathrm{g}$ with a retention of $84.1\%$ (the maximum of $1071.9~\mathrm{{mAh}/\Omega}$ g) after 300 cycles, which reveals that $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ effectively enhanced energy storage performance. Additionally, the coulombic efficiency consistently remained at $99\%$ during the cycle tests, which further confirmed the favorable reversibility of $5{\mathsf{C o}}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}/{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ . In addition, XRD and TEM results demonstrated the robust structure and integrity of $5{\mathsf{C o}}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}/{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ after 300 cycles (Fig. S7 and S8).
To understand the lithium storage mechanism of $5{\mathsf{C o}}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}/{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ CV tests at different scan rates were conducted to study the electrochemical kinetics. Fig. 7a shows that despite increasing the sweep rate, the shapes of the CV curves were maintained, which indicated excellent reversibility. The cathodic/anodic peaks shifted to the left/right, respectively. In addition, the scan rate (m) and current (i) satisfy the following equations [59]:
$$
\begin{array}{l}{i=\mathbf{a}\nu^{\mathrm{b}}}\\ {i=\mathsf{k}_{1}\nu+\mathsf{k}_{2}\nu^{1/2}}\end{array}
$$
For electrode materials, the value of b is calculated by formula (1) to determine whether the composite exhibits pseudocapacitive behavior during the charging-discharging process. When b is 0.5, the electrode exhibits battery behavior based on a diffusioncontrolled process, while a value of 1.0 suggests surfacecontrolled behavior based on a capacitive process [60,61]. Fig. 7b depicts that the b values of the cathodic and anodic peaks were 0.80 and 0.63, respectively. This revealed that both surface- and diffusion-controlled processes took place in $5{\mathsf{C o}}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}/{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ during the cycling process. To understand the contribution of capacitive behavior at different sweep rates, equation (2) was used. Fig. 7c shows that with increasing scan rate, the capacitive contribution increased from $61.3\%$ to $78.0\%$ . This was the main reason for the excellent $5{\mathsf{C o}}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}/{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ rate performance. The detailed pseudocapacitive contributions in Fig. 7d clearly show the high capacitive contribution $(78\%)$ at $1.0~\mathrm{mV}/s$ , which indicates that $5{\mathsf{C o}}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}/{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ is controlled by pseudocapacitive behavior. This prompted the fast-kinetic process and the excellent rate capability. The refinement of $5{\mathsf{C o}}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}/{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ during the cycling process resulted in a shortened ion diffusion path and more exposed active sites [62].
Moreover, to investigate the effect of ${\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ on Li-ion diffusion in the electrode materials, the galvanostatic intermittent titration technique (GITT) was used to analyze the Li-ion diffusion coefficients $(\mathsf{D}_{\mathtt{L i}+})$ in $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and $5{\mathsf{C o}}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}/{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ during the chargingdischarging processes (Eq. S1). Fig. S9 shows that $\mathsf{D}_{\mathsf{L i^{+}}}$ varied during the charging-discharging process. The minimal value appeared at each redox plateau, where $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ severely diffused into/from the crystalline framework [63]. It can be observed in Fig. 8 that the $\mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{Li^{+}}}$ of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ ranged from $1.92\times10^{-12}$ to $6.00\times10^{-10}\mathrm{cm}^{2}/s$ during the first discharging process and from $1.92\times10^{-11}$ to $2.15\times10^{-9}\mathrm{cm}^{2}/$ s during the first charging process. However, the $\mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{Li^{+}}}$ of $5C_{0_{3}}0_{4}/$ $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ ( $1.22\times10^{-12}$ to $6.31\times10^{-10}\mathrm{cm}^{2}/s$ during the first discharging process and $3.01\times10^{-11}$ to $4.04\times10^{-9}\mathrm{cm}^{2}/s$ during the first charging process) and $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ are similar. At a delithiation state of
$20\%$ during the charging process, $5{\mathsf{C o}}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}/{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ provided a higher $\mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{Li^{+}}}$ $\langle1.51\times10^{-9}\mathrm{cm}^{2}/s\rangle$ than $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ $(\mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{Li^{+}}}=8.21\times10^{-10}\mathrm{cm}^{2}/s)$ The higher $\mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{Li^{+}}}$ value was attributed to the nanocrystalline domains, which efficiently promoted the diffusion of $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ , resulting in a shorter solid-phase diffusion length [64,65]. Moreover, the results show that the addition of ${\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ as a semiconductor material does not affect the diffusion properties of ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ in $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ .
## 4. Conclusions
Herein, $\mathrm{Co_{3}O_{4}}/\mathrm{CeO_{2}}$ composites were prepared using a microwave-assisted solvothermal method followed by subsequent pyrolysis of MOF precursors, which enhanced the lithium storage behavior. The unique heterostructure allows for the combination of the $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ and $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ advantages, provides a buffered space to accommodate the volume change, and shortens the ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ diffusion path during the lithiation-delithiation process. Specifically, $5{\mathsf{C o}}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}/{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ shows an initial discharge capacity of $1090.1~\mathrm{mAh/g}$ and a high reversible capacity of $1131.2\mathrm{mAh/g}$ , with a current density of $100\mathrm{\mA/g}$ after 100 cycles. The rate data indicate that $5{\mathsf{C o}}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}/{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ exhibits a discharge capacity of $835.3\mathrm{\mAh/g}$ at a current density of $2000~\mathrm{{mA/g}}$ . The excellent ${\mathrm{Li}}^{+}$ storage capability is attributed to the conversion reaction and interfacial capacitance contribution. To the best of our knowledge, among all $\mathrm{Co}_{3}0_{4}.$ based composites, $5{\mathsf{C o}}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}/{\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ exhibits excellent rate capacity and high efficiency [55–57,66]. Therefore, these novel ${C o_{3}}{0_{4}}/$ ${\mathsf{C e O}}_{2}$ composites are promising anodes for next-generation LIBs.
